"Cost: the vocabulary must stay small and stable — aliases absorb common words (worker, database, emits)."],"criteria":[]},"sections":[{"id":"A","title":"Decision"},{"id":"B","title":"Context"},{"id":"C","title":"Options"},{"id":"D","title":"Consequences"}],"text":"Decision span=3 Describe the system as elements of about twenty kinds (service, store, broker, contract, entity, flow, question…). Connect them with typed frontmatter lists ( calls , exposes , uses , publishes , consumes , reads , writes , owns , decided by , blocked by …). A connection to an interface is a contract reference transport:name ; a reference creates the contract, a contract file adds detail. Context span=3 - Agents need to answer \"how does X reach Y\" by walking hops, not by grepping. - A bare arrow loses the protocol; a separate interface file for every endpoint is too much work. - Publisher and consumer are written by different teams and should not have to name each other. Options span=3 Option Pros Cons Fit --- --- --- --- A — Typed lists + contract refs, derived edges Each side writes its own fact. Topics connect producers and consumers automatically. A vocabulary to learn. ok B — Free arrows with labels Easy to write. No protocol, no checks, no derived links. no C — Full C4 / Structurizr model Rich and known. A second DSL; far from the code; no decisions or flows. warn Consequences span=3 - Positive: lint can check flow hops and contract sides; the map and element pages need no extra input. - Cost: the vocabulary must stay small and stable — aliases absorb common words ( worker , database , emits )."},"source":"---\ntitle: Elements with typed connections; contracts as transport:name\nstatus: Accepted\ndate: 2026-10-06\nauthor: abtin\ntags: [format, graph]\ncomponents: [graph]\ndepends_on: [0001]\napplies_to: [internal/adr/**, skills/crux/reference.md]\n---\n\n## Decision {span=3}\n```callout ok Decision\nDescribe the system as elements of about twenty kinds (service, store, broker, contract, entity, flow, question…). Connect them with typed frontmatter lists (`calls`, `exposes`, `uses`, `publishes`, `consumes`, `reads`, `writes`, `owns`, `decided_by`, `blocked_by`…). A connection to an interface is a contract reference `transport:name`; a reference creates the contract, a contract file adds detail.\n```\n\n## Context {span=3}\n- Agents need to answer \"how does X reach Y\" by walking hops, not by grepping.\n- A bare arrow loses the protocol; a separate interface file for every endpoint is too much work.\n- Publisher and consumer are written by different teams and should not have to name each other.\n\n## Options {span=3}\n| Option | Pros | Cons | Fit |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| A — Typed lists + contract refs, derived edges | Each side writes its own fact. Topics connect producers and consumers automatically. | A vocabulary to learn. | ok |\n| B — Free arrows with labels | Easy to write. | No protocol, no checks, no derived links. | no |\n| C — Full C4 / Structurizr model | Rich and known. | A second DSL; far from the code; no decisions or flows. | warn |\n\n## Consequences {span=3}\n- Positive: lint can check flow hops and contract sides; the map and element pages need no extra input.\n- Cost: the vocabulary must stay small and stable — aliases absorb common words (`worker`, `database`, `emits`).\n"}